    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "B, d, g: it depends on the word",
     "texto": [
      "The letters **b, d, g** don't have a fixed equivalent among English consonants. In some positions they get weaker; in others, as in **glad**, the d is soft.\n\nDouble consonants usually show that the vowel before them is short; they are not pronounced as two separate hits. In **lykke**, «kk» is pronounced as a soft consonant, not as two separate, strong «k» sounds."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "tabla",
     "titulo": "Consonants with a trick",
     "columnas": [
      {
       "texto": "Letter",
       "lengua": "alumno"
      },
      {
       "texto": "What happens",
       "lengua": "alumno"
      },
      {
       "texto": "Example",
       "lengua": "alumno"
      }
     ],
     "filas": [
      [
       {
        "texto": "**d** at the end",
        "lengua": "alumno"
       },
       {
        "texto": "can sound soft, like the **th** in «this»",
        "lengua": "alumno"
       },
       {
        "texto": "glad",
        "lengua": "estudio"
       }
      ],
      [
       {
        "texto": "**d** silent",
        "lengua": "alumno"
       },
       {
        "texto": "in some groups it isn't heard as a separate consonant; check each word",
        "lengua": "alumno"
       },
       {
        "texto": "kold, land, bord",
        "lengua": "estudio"
       }
      ],
      [
       {
        "texto": "**g** at the end",
        "lengua": "alumno"
       },
       {
        "texto": "it weakens; in «læge» it sounds like a short «y»",
        "lengua": "alumno"
       },
       {
        "texto": "pige, læge",
        "lengua": "estudio"
       }
      ],
      [
       {
        "texto": "**h**",
        "lengua": "estudio"
       },
       {
        "texto": "sounds like a soft breath; it is silent before v or j",
        "lengua": "alumno"
       },
       {
        "texto": "hus · hvem, hvor",
        "lengua": "estudio"
       }
      ],
      [
       {
        "texto": "**k**",
        "lengua": "alumno"
       },
       {
        "texto": "it is pronounced in «kniv», unlike in English «knife»",
        "lengua": "alumno"
       },
       {
        "texto": "kniv",
        "lengua": "estudio"
       }
      ],
      [
       {
        "texto": "**r**",
        "lengua": "alumno"
       },
       {
        "texto": "in the throat, like the French r",
        "lengua": "alumno"
       },
       {
        "texto": "—",
        "lengua": "estudio"
       }
      ],
      [
       {
        "texto": "**t**",
        "lengua": "alumno"
       },
       {
        "texto": "it is hardly heard in «det»; the pronunciation of «at» varies according to its function and context",
        "lengua": "alumno"
       },
       {
        "texto": "det, at",
        "lengua": "estudio"
       }
      ],
      [
       {
        "texto": "**v**",
        "lengua": "alumno"
       },
       {
        "texto": "it isn't heard as a separate consonant in «selv» and «gulv»",
        "lengua": "alumno"
       },
       {
        "texto": "selv, gulv",
        "lengua": "estudio"
       }
      ]
     ],
     "nota": "",
     "tutora": "pensando"
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "The r",
     "texto": [
      "The Danish r is articulated **at the back, in the throat**; it doesn't sound like the English r, and it changes the nearby vowel."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "The stød",
     "texto": [
      "The **stød** is a brief constriction or creaky voice on a stressed syllable; it isn't a consonant that is always written. For example, it appears in **hus**, **kold**, **bord** and **to**, but not in **hedder**.\n\nDon't worry: if you don't do it yet, people will still understand you. Listen to it and copy it little by little."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Stress and intonation",
     "texto": [
      "- In many words the main stress falls on the **first syllable**, as in English; some prefixes like **be-**, **ge-** and **er-** are usually unstressed.\n- Some loanwords put the stress later: stati**on**, universi**tet**, tu**rist**.\n- In **hvordan** and **hvornår** the stress is often heard on the second syllable; **hvorfor** allows variants.\n\nIntonation depends on the type of sentence and on which word you emphasise; it isn't always flat or falling."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    }
   ]
  },
